  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 158 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nOn Thursday and Friday, the upper-level pattern swings from a\nzonal flow to mostly northwesterly as the trough starts\nsliding eastward before a weakening ridge over northern \nNebraska moves to the southeast. This should allow a slight \nwarming trend with afternoon highs as temperatures are forecast \nto rise from upper 40s and low 50s on Thursday to upper 50s on \nFriday. However, morning lows are still expected to be at or \njust above freezing before dawn. Clouds are also expected to \ndecrease over these two days with winds shifting from the south \nto the southwest as a weak surface high pressure slides in from \nthe west.\n\nOver the weekend, it is expected that a broad trough system\nwould follow through the area on Saturday and start to dig to\nthe southeast. At the same time, a secondary ridge pattern\nstarts to form to the west and amplify to the northeast. With\nour area in between these two features, we can expect a cold\nfront to pass through and could allow a small chance (15%) for\nrain overnight Saturday and into Sunday morning. With this, winds\nare forecast to pick up from the north being between 15 and 20\nmph. There is some uncertainty on Sunday regarding the upper-\nlevel pattern as ensemble spread increases to the southwest and\ncould push the core of the broad trough into Texas. If this does\nhappen, then this would allow the upper-level pattern to be more\nnortherly and could bring in even more cooler air than\nanticipated on Sunday. There is low confidence (20%) that this\nwould happen. Otherwise, temperatures are expected to be\nsimilar to Thursday and Friday.",
